  "summary": "Opinion column by constitutional attorney John Whitehead arguing that Flock Safety's AI-powered ALPR network, combined with data fusion and public-private partnerships, constitutes an unconstitutional mass surveillance system. Whitehead cites the Supreme Court's Chatrie decision as potential constitutional grounding for challenges and notes some cities have terminated Flock contracts.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "License plate cameras now log approximately 20 billion vehicle scans every month.",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    },
    {
      "quote": "The government should not be permitted to accomplish through Flock what it could not constitutionally accomplish by assigning police officers to follow millions of Americans everywhere they drive.",
      "paragraph_idx": 32
    },
    {
      "quote": "Some cities have terminated or declined to renew their Flock contracts. Others have paused deployments or demanded stronger restrictions on data sharing, retention and federal access.",
      "paragraph_idx": 42
    }
  ],
